\title{Synergy and Overlap in Batteries of Measurement Dials:\\ An Exact Co-Information Calculus}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the information-theoretic behaviour of \emph{batteries}: finite collections of deterministic measurement instruments (``dials''), each applied to the same finite population, and each evaluated by how much it reveals about a hidden label. A natural and frequently assumed hypothesis --- that dials built from structurally unrelated ingredients (for instance, residue readings at coprime conductors) contribute \emph{additively} to the joint capacity of the battery --- is false, and we show that it fails in both directions. We prove an exact identity governing the discrepancy: for two dials $f,g$ read against a label $L$ on a finite population,
\[
\Delta(L;f,g) := I(L;(f,g)) - I(L;f) - I(L;g) = I(f;g\mid L) - I(f;g).
\]
The failure of additivity is precisely the excess of the label-conditional dependence of the two readouts over their unconditional dependence. From this identity we derive: nonnegativity of the conditional dependence (from a data-processing argument, not assumed); the sharp overlap bound $-\Delta \le I(f;g)$; the marginal bound $-\Delta \le \min(I(L;f),I(L;g))$; the ceiling $\Delta \le \min(H(f),H(g))$; the corollary that unconditionally independent readouts are necessarily super-additive; a knife-edge characterisation of exact additivity; and the total-overlap theorem $\Delta = -I(L;g)$ when one dial is a post-processing of the other. We exhibit a single three-dial battery on a four-element population whose rows realise $+1$, $0$ and $-1$ bit, establishing that the battery space is neither additive nor comonotone and that both derived bounds are attained. We then widen the law to arbitrary width $k$ via total correlation, obtaining the $k$-dial overlap bound, its attainment at every width, and super-additivity of independent batteries at every width. Algorithms, numerical demonstrations and applications to feature selection, sensor fusion and interaction analysis are given.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} co-information, synergy, redundancy, total correlation, mutual information, feature selection, conditional independence, data processing inequality.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivating measurements}

Consider a finite population $\Omega$ of arithmetic objects --- say pairs $(p,q)$ of primes drawn from a fixed index range --- carrying a hidden \emph{label} $L(x)$ that one wishes to predict. A \emph{dial} is a deterministic instrument: a function $f:\Omega\to\mathbb{Z}/m$ returning a residue, for instance the splitting behaviour of $p$ in a fixed number field of conductor $m$. Its \emph{capacity} is the mutual information $I(L;f)$, measured here in bits.

Batteries of such dials were measured against a fixed population and a fixed label, with the following results (bits):

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
battery & $I(\text{joint})$ & $I_1+I_2$ & $\Delta$\\
\midrule
cubic at conductor $31$ $\times$ cubic at conductor $23$ & $2.1314$ & $2.0024$ & $+0.129$ (synergy)\\
quartic at $9$ $\times$ dihedral at $8$ & $1.9125$ & $1.9076$ & $+0.005$ (near-additive)\\
two cubics of shared discriminant $-23$ & $1.0104$ & $2.0024$ & $-0.992$ (overlap)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The pre-registered hypothesis --- \emph{coprime conductors imply additive capacity} --- is refuted at the first pair, and the refutation is two-sided. Each individual marginal had been independently re-verified against earlier measurement campaigns ($1.0012$, $1.0012$, $0.4733$, $1.4342$ bits) \emph{before} any joint measurement was taken, so the discrepancies cannot be attributed to drift in the marginals. A further diagnostic --- a ``which-factor wall'' checking that no joint channel leaks information about which factor of the population an individual came from --- stayed below $0.0016$ bits across every joint channel, so the synergised and overlapping content is symmetric and factor-blind.

\subsection{The reasoning error and its correction}

The additivity argument implicitly treats the two dial readouts as \emph{independent draws}. They are not: both dials are evaluated at the same individual $x\in\Omega$. Consequently their readouts are correlated through the population, and moreover the joint readout can express combinations of residues --- seeing $p \bmod m_1$ and $q \bmod m_2$ simultaneously --- that neither marginal can express. This paper shows that this informal mechanism is an exact theorem: the discrepancy $\Delta$ is the difference of two nonnegative dependence terms of opposite effect, and additivity requires their exact cancellation, a codimension-one condition on the joint law.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The pair co-information identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo}) and its complete consequence set (Theorems~\ref{thm:condnonneg}--\ref{thm:refine}).
\item A minimal witness realising $+1$, $0$, $-1$ bits in one three-dial battery on four individuals (Section~\ref{sec:witness}).
\item The width-$k$ law with total correlation, nonnegativity of both terms, the overlap bound and its sharpness at every width (Section~\ref{sec:widthk}).
\item Algorithms and numerics (Sections~\ref{sec:algos}--\ref{sec:numerics}).
\end{enumerate}
All results hold for arbitrary finite populations, arbitrary finite-valued labels and arbitrary deterministic dials; nothing is arithmetic-specific.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set, the \emph{population}, with the uniform distribution. (Uniformity is a convenience; every identity below is an identity of entropies and holds for any fixed distribution.) Logarithms in definitions are natural; quantities in \emph{bits} are divided by $\log 2$.

\begin{definition}[Statistic and entropy]
A \emph{statistic} is a function $f:\Omega\to A$. Its entropy is
\[
H(f) = -\sum_{a\in f(\Omega)} \frac{|f^{-1}(a)|}{|\Omega|}\log\frac{|f^{-1}(a)|}{|\Omega|}.
\]
$H(f)$ depends only on the partition of $\Omega$ into fibres of $f$. We call this the \emph{fibre principle}: if $f(x)=f(y)\iff f'(x)=f'(y)$ for all $x,y$, then $H(f)=H(f')$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pairing]
For $f:\Omega\to A$ and $g:\Omega\to B$, the pair statistic is $(f,g):x\mapsto (f(x),g(x))$; similarly for triples. By the fibre principle, entropies of pairs and triples are invariant under reordering and re-bracketing of components.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Mutual and conditional information]
$I(f;g)=H(f)+H(g)-H((f,g))$ and $H(g\mid L)=H((L,g))-H(L)$. One has $I(f;g)\ge 0$ and $0\le H(g\mid L)\le H(g)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dial, battery, capacity]
A \emph{dial} is a pair $(m,f)$ with $f:\Omega\to\{0,\dots,m-1\}$; we identify a dial with its readout. A \emph{battery} is a finite family $(f_i)_{i\in\iota}$, and for $S\subseteq\iota$ the \emph{joint readout} is $J_S : x\mapsto (f_i(x))_{i\in S}$. The \emph{capacity} is $\Cap(S)=I(L;J_S)$ and the \emph{measured synergy} in bits is
\[
\Syn(S) = \frac{1}{\log 2}\Big( I(L;J_S) - \sum_{i\in S} I(L;f_i)\Big).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pair synergy and overlap]
$\Delta(L;f,g)=I(L;(f,g))-I(L;f)-I(L;g)$ and $\Ovl(L;f,g)=-\Delta(L;f,g)$. Positive $\Delta$ is \emph{synergy}; positive $\Ovl$ is \emph{overlap}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The two dependence terms]
The \emph{unconditional readout dependence} (shared channel) is $\Dep(f,g)=I(f;g)$. The \emph{label-conditional dependence} is
\[
\Dep_L(f,g) = H((L,f)) + H((L,g)) - H((L,f,g)) - H(L),
\]
equivalently $\Dep_L(f,g)=H(g\mid L)-H(g\mid (L,f))$.
\end{definition}

For the arithmetic batteries above, $\Dep(f,g)$ has a concrete meaning: for two cubic fields of the same discriminant it is at least the information carried by their common quadratic character, the channel through which the overlap flows.

\section{The co-information identity}

\begin{theorem}[Pair co-information identity]\label{thm:coinfo}
For any finite population, any label $L$ and any dials $f,g$,
\[
\Delta(L;f,g) = \Dep_L(f,g) - \Dep(f,g),
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
I(L;(f,g))-I(L;f)-I(L;g) = I(f;g\mid L) - I(f;g).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand every mutual information into entropies and use the fibre principle to identify $(L,(f,g))$ with $(L,f,g)$. Both sides reduce to the same alternating sum
\[
H((L,f)) + H((L,g)) + H((f,g)) - H(L) - H(f) - H(g) - H((L,f,g)),
\]
so the identity is an algebraic rearrangement.
\end{proof}

The displayed symmetric expression is the negative of the classical co-information of the triple $(L,f,g)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo} says the additivity defect of a two-dial battery \emph{is} that co-information.

\begin{remark}
Additivity is the claim $\Delta=0$, which requires \emph{two} quantities to cancel. The coprime-conductor argument reasons only about $\Dep(f,g)$, and silently assumes $\Dep_L$ vanishes too. But $\Dep=0$ forces $\Delta=\Dep_L\ge 0$, so the corrected argument predicts strict super-additivity generically (Theorem~\ref{thm:indep}).
\end{remark}

\section{Nonnegativity of the conditional dependence}

\begin{lemma}[Reshuffling]\label{lem:shuffle}
$H\big((g,(L,f))\big)=H\big((L,f,g)\big)$ and $H((g,L))=H((L,g))$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both are instances of the fibre principle: the statistics on each side agree on a pair of individuals exactly when all listed components agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Conditional dependence as an uncertainty drop]\label{lem:drop}
$\Dep_L(f,g)=H(g\mid L)-H(g\mid (L,f))$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unfold both conditional entropies and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:shuffle}; the $H(L)$ and $H((L,f))$ terms cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional dependence is nonnegative]\label{thm:condnonneg}
$\Dep_L(f,g)\ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Conditioning on a finer statistic cannot increase conditional entropy: if $u=\phi\circ v$, then $H(g\mid v)\le H(g\mid u)$. Apply this with $v=(L,f)$ and $u=L$, the first-coordinate post-processing of $v$, and conclude by Lemma~\ref{lem:drop}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Symmetry and ceilings]\label{prop:sym}
$\Dep_L(f,g)=\Dep_L(g,f)$ and $\Dep_L(f,g)\le\min\{H(f),H(g)\}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Symmetry is the fibre principle applied to $(L,f,g)$ versus $(L,g,f)$. For the ceiling, Lemma~\ref{lem:drop} gives $\Dep_L\le H(g\mid L)\le H(g)$, using $H(g\mid(L,f))\ge0$; the other bound follows by symmetry.
\end{proof}

\section{The two directions of the refutation}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap is bounded by the shared channel]\label{thm:ovlbound}
$\Ovl(L;f,g)=I_1+I_2-I(\text{joint})\le I(f;g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Negate Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo}: $\Ovl=\Dep-\Dep_L$, and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:condnonneg}.
\end{proof}

This is the quantitative form of ``shared conductor structure makes dials comonotone'': two cubic dials of discriminant $-23$ can overlap by $0.992$ bits only because their readouts share at least that much.

\begin{theorem}[Independent readouts can only synergize]\label{thm:indep}
If $I(f;g)=0$ then $\Delta(L;f,g)\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

Transversality of the instruments does not yield additivity; it yields super-additivity, with excess equal to the conditional dependence. The measured $+0.129$ is therefore the expected sign; the interesting question is the magnitude of $\Dep_L$, which is structure-dependent (rich-type pairs $+0.129$, lossy-type pairs $+0.005$).

\begin{theorem}[Additivity is a knife edge]\label{thm:knife}
$\Delta(L;f,g)=0$ if and only if $\Dep_L(f,g)=\Dep(f,g)$.
\end{theorem}

Exact additivity is one scalar equation on the joint law of $(L,f,g)$: a codimension-one, hence non-generic, condition, not implied by any structural transversality hypothesis.

\begin{theorem}[Synergy ceiling]\label{thm:synceil}
$\Delta(L;f,g)\le\min\{H(f),H(g)\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Delta=\Dep_L-\Dep\le\Dep_L$ since $\Dep\ge0$; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:sym}.
\end{proof}

\section{Monotonicity and the marginal overlap bound}

\begin{lemma}[Joint dominance]\label{lem:jointdom}
$I(L;f)\le I(L;(f,g))$ and $I(L;g)\le I(L;(f,g))$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$f$ and $g$ are coordinate post-processings of $(f,g)$; apply the data processing inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap never exceeds the smaller marginal]\label{thm:minbound}
$\Ovl(L;f,g)\le\min\{I(L;f),I(L;g)\}$, equivalently $\Delta\ge-\min\{I_1,I_2\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $I_1\le I_2$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:jointdom} $I(\text{joint})\ge I_2$, so $\Delta\ge -I_1$; the other case is symmetric.
\end{proof}

In the measured table the two $-23$ cubics have $I_1=I_2=1.0012$ bits and overlap $0.992$ bits: $99.1\%$ of this bound. Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:synceil} and \ref{thm:minbound} gives the two-sided law
\[
-\min\{I(L;f),I(L;g)\} \;\le\; \Delta(L;f,g) \;\le\; \min\{H(f),H(g)\}.
\]

\section{``Same subfield = same dial'': total overlap}

\begin{theorem}[A refining dial contributes nothing]\label{thm:refine}
If $g=u\circ f$ for some function $u$, then $I(L;(f,g))=I(L;f)$ and $\Delta(L;f,g)=-I(L;g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fibres of $(f,u\circ f)$ coincide with those of $f$, so by the fibre principle $I(L;(f,g))=I(L;f)$; substitute into the definition of $\Delta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Comonotone extreme]
$\Ovl(L;f,f)=I(L;f)$: a duplicated dial costs exactly a full marginal.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Theorem~\ref{thm:ovlbound} is sharp]
With $g=f$ and $L=f$, $\Ovl(f;f,f)=I(f;f)=H(f)$, so no better universal bound of that form exists.
\end{corollary}

\section{A minimal witness: all three signs in one battery}\label{sec:witness}

\begin{construction}
Let $\Omega=\{0,1\}^2$, uniformly weighted. Define three dials of modulus $2$: dial $0$ reads $f_0(b_1,b_2)=b_1$; dial $1$ reads $f_1(b_1,b_2)=b_2$; dial $2$ is a deliberate duplicate $f_2=f_0$. Define two labels: the parity $L_\oplus=b_1\oplus b_2$ and the first-bit label $L_1=b_1$.
\end{construction}

\begin{lemma}[Marginals on four individuals]
$H(f_0)=H(f_1)=H(L_\oplus)=H(L_1)=1$ bit, and each of $(L_\oplus,f_0)$, $(L_\oplus,f_1)$, $(L_1,f_1)$, $(f_0,f_1)$ separates all four individuals, hence has entropy $2$ bits.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:caps}
$I(L_\oplus;f_0)=I(L_\oplus;f_1)=0$; $I(L_1;f_1)=0$; $I(L_1;f_0)=1$; $I(L_\oplus;(f_0,f_1))=1$; $I(L_1;(f_0,f_1))=1$ (bits).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The vanishing ones are $1+1-2=0$. The others follow since the relevant statistic determines the label, and then the mutual information equals the label entropy, here $1$ bit.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three rows, three signs]\label{thm:rows}
$\Delta(L_\oplus;f_0,f_1)=+1$ bit (both dials individually blind to parity, jointly pinning it, attaining the label-entropy ceiling); $\Delta(L_1;f_0,f_1)=0$ (exact additivity); $\Delta(L_1;f_0,f_2)=-1$ bit (total overlap by duplication).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The identity accounts for both signs]
In the synergy row $\Dep(f_0,f_1)=0$ and $\Dep_{L_\oplus}(f_0,f_1)=1$ bit; in the overlap row $\Dep(f_0,f_2)=1$ bit and $\Dep_{L_1}(f_0,f_2)=0$. Both satisfy $\Delta=\Dep_L-\Dep$.
\end{theorem}

Thus $+1$ is pure conditional coupling with no shared channel, and $-1$ is pure shared channel with no conditional coupling: the two engines of Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo} isolated one at a time.

\begin{theorem}[Neither additive nor comonotone]
There is no valid universal inequality $\Delta\ge 0$ and none of the form $\Delta\le 0$ for two-dial batteries --- not even after fixing the population and the dial family. Moreover the overlap row saturates both derived bounds simultaneously:
\[
\Ovl(L_1;f_0,f_2)=\min\{I(L_1;f_0),I(L_1;f_2)\}=I(f_0;f_2)=1 \text{ bit}.
\]
\end{theorem}

Note the extra force of the example: the \emph{same} pair $\{0,1\}$ is strictly super-additive against one label and exactly additive against another. Whether a battery synergizes is a joint property of instruments and target, not of the instruments alone.

\section{The width-$k$ law}\label{sec:widthk}

\begin{definition}[Total correlation]
For finite $S$ with joint readout $J_S$,
\[
\TC(S)=\sum_{i\in S}H(f_i)-H(J_S),\qquad
\TC_L(S)=\sum_{i\in S}H(f_i\mid L)-H(J_S\mid L).
\]
$\TC$ vanishes exactly when the readouts are jointly independent.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Width-$k$ synergy]
$\Delta(S)=I(L;J_S)-\sum_{i\in S}I(L;f_i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Conditional subadditivity]\label{lem:condsub}
$H((u,v)\mid L)\le H(u\mid L)+H(v\mid L)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
After unfolding and reshuffling, the inequality is literally $\Dep_L(u,v)\ge0$, i.e.\ Theorem~\ref{thm:condnonneg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Both correlation terms are nonnegative]\label{thm:tcnonneg}
$\TC(S)\ge0$ and $\TC_L(S)\ge0$ for every finite $S$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $S$. For $S=\emptyset$ the joint readout is constant and both terms vanish. For $S\to S\cup\{a\}$: the joint readout of $S\cup\{a\}$ has the same fibres as the pair $(f_a,J_S)$, since agreeing on all coordinates of $S\cup\{a\}$ is the same as agreeing on $f_a$ and on all coordinates of $S$. Unconditional subadditivity $H((f_a,J_S))\le H(f_a)+H(J_S)$ gives the step for $\TC$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:condsub} gives it for $\TC_L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Width-$k$ co-information law]\label{thm:widthk}
For every finite $S$, $\Delta(S)=\TC_L(S)-\TC(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $H(f_i\mid L)=H((L,f_i))-H(L)$ and $H(J_S\mid L)=H((L,J_S))-H(L)$, using reshuffling to order components, and $I(L;f_i)=H(L)+H(f_i)-H((L,f_i))$. Substituting, the $|S|$ copies of $H(L)$ from the sum cancel against those in $\TC_L$, and the remaining terms match pairwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$k$-dial overlap bound]\label{thm:kovl}
$\Delta(S)\ge -\TC(S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Independent batteries are super-additive at every width]
If $\TC(S)=0$ then $\Delta(S)\ge0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Synergy ceiling at width $k$]
$\Delta(S)\le \TC_L(S)$: the joint readout can extract at most what the label classes still correlate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness at every width]\label{thm:sharp}
Let every dial in a nonempty $S$ read the same statistic $u$, and let the label be $u$ itself. Then $\TC(S)=(|S|-1)H(u)$ and $\Delta(S)=-(|S|-1)H(u)=-\TC(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The joint readout has the same fibres as $u$, so $H(J_S)=H(u)$ and $I(u;J_S)=H(u)$, while each marginal capacity is $H(u)$; substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-sided width-$k$ law]
$-\TC(S)\le\Delta(S)\le\TC_L(S)$, with the left inequality attained by duplicated batteries at every width.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algos}

Let $n=|\Omega|$ and $k=|S|$. All quantities are computable exactly by fibre counting.

\paragraph{Empirical entropy.} Group individuals by readout and sum $-\hat p\log\hat p$: cost $O(n)$ time and $O(|f(\Omega)|)$ memory.

\paragraph{Pair decomposition.} Compute $H(L),H(f),H(g),H((L,f)),H((L,g)),H((f,g)),H((L,f,g))$ and assemble $I_1,I_2,I(\text{joint}),\Delta,\Dep,\Dep_L$. Cost $O(n)$ (seven linear passes). The identity $\Delta=\Dep_L-\Dep$ serves as an exact runtime check up to floating-point rounding.

\paragraph{Width-$k$ decomposition.} Compute the $k$ marginal passes, the joint pass and the label-joint pass; assemble $I(L;J_S)$, $\sum_i I(L;f_i)$, $\TC$, $\TC_L$, $\Delta$, and check $\Delta=\TC_L-\TC$. Cost $O(kn)$ time, $O(n)$ memory in the worst case.

\paragraph{Greedy battery construction.} When assembling a battery from a catalogue, the marginal capacity of a candidate bounds its contribution only after subtracting overlap with what is already installed. Selecting at each step the candidate maximising the increment $I(L;(J,f))-I(L;J)$ is the corrected procedure: by Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo}, applied with the installed joint readout in place of $f$, that increment equals $I(L;f)+\Dep_L(J,f)-\Dep(J,f)$. Cost $O(k\cdot|\text{catalogue}|\cdot n)$.

\section{Numerical demonstrations}\label{sec:numerics}

Exact computations on small populations reproduce each structural phenomenon of the measured table (values in bits).

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Pure synergy (parity).} $\Omega=\{0,1\}^2$, $L=b_1\oplus b_2$: $I_1=I_2=0$, $I(\text{joint})=1$, $\Delta=+1$, $\Dep=0$, $\Dep_L=1$.
\item \textbf{Exact additivity.} Same dials, $L=b_1$: $I_1=1$, $I_2=0$, $I(\text{joint})=1$, $\Delta=0$, $\Dep=\Dep_L=0$.
\item \textbf{Total overlap.} $L=b_1$, dials $f_0$ and its duplicate: $I_1=I_2=1$, $I(\text{joint})=1$, $\Delta=-1$, $\Dep=1$, $\Dep_L=0$.
\item \textbf{Partial overlap.} With $f_0=(b_1,b_2)$, $f_1=(b_2,b_3)$ sharing a middle bit and $L=b_1\oplus b_3$, both engines are active and $\Delta$ interpolates: the generic regime of the measured $+0.129$ and $+0.005$ rows.
\item \textbf{Width-$k$ duplication.} For $k$ copies of a fair bit read against that bit, $\TC=k-1$ and $\Delta=-(k-1)$ exactly, matching Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.
\item \textbf{Random batteries.} Sampling random dials on random populations, $\Delta$ is supported on both signs with exact zeros only at coincidences, the statistical sense of Theorem~\ref{thm:knife}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the identity decides}
Three consequences follow for the measurement programme. First, product batteries are covered \emph{with their synergy excesses included}: a battery of $k$ dials delivers more than $k$ times a marginal for structurally rich families and less for shared-structure families, and both deviations are computed by the same joint machinery. Second, overlap is always auditable: Theorems~\ref{thm:ovlbound} and \ref{thm:kovl} bound double-counting by a label-free observable of the raw readouts. Third, redundancy has an exact signature: Theorem~\ref{thm:refine} makes ``same subfield = same dial'' a theorem, and the observed $0.992$ against a bound of $1.0012$ locates the two $-23$ cubics at $99.1\%$ of that extreme.

\subsection{Relation to classical information theory}
The functional $\Dep_L-\Dep$ is, up to sign conventions, the classical interaction information (co-information) of a triple, and total correlation is likewise classical. The contribution here is the identification of a measurement battery's additivity defect \emph{with} those functionals, the derivation of the full set of two-sided bounds in that language, the simultaneous realisation of all extreme values in a four-element population, and the exact extension to arbitrary width with attainment at every width. Theorem~\ref{thm:condnonneg} is \emph{derived} from a data-processing argument rather than posited, which matters because nonnegativity of unconditional and of conditional mutual information are logically distinct facts --- conflating them is exactly the error in the additivity argument. We stress the asymmetry that makes the subject non-trivial: $\Dep\ge0$ and $\Dep_L\ge0$, but their difference is unsigned.

\subsection{Limitations}
The results are exact statements about empirical entropies on finite populations with deterministic dials. Nothing here addresses estimation: plug-in entropy estimators are biased downward, and the bias in $\Delta$ is a difference of biases of different orders, the joint term having the largest alphabet. A measured small positive $\Delta$ (the $+0.005$ row) is not by itself evidence of synergy without a bias analysis; the $+0.129$ and $-0.992$ rows are far outside any plausible bias scale. The bounds are universal and attained, but they are not predictions for a particular arithmetic family; predicting $\Dep_L$ from Galois-theoretic type remains open.

\subsection{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Feature selection.} Ranking features by marginal mutual information and keeping the top $k$ is unsound in both directions: it double-counts redundant features and discards individually uninformative but jointly decisive ones (the parity witness is the minimal counterexample). The greedy increment procedure of Section~\ref{sec:algos} is the correction.
\item \textbf{Sensor fusion and experimental design.} Theorem~\ref{thm:ovlbound} gives a label-free pre-audit of redundancy; Theorem~\ref{thm:kovl} extends it to whole arrays.
\item \textbf{Interaction and epistasis analysis.} ``Neither factor alone, both together'' is $\Delta>0$, localised by Theorem~\ref{thm:coinfo} as conditional dependence exceeding unconditional dependence --- an operational test distinguishing genuine interaction from shared confounding.
\item \textbf{Ensemble design.} The preference for diverse members is $\TC\approx0$, and diversity guarantees super-additivity at every width.
\item \textbf{Privacy and leakage auditing.} The same calculus bounds how much a collection of released statistics can jointly reveal beyond the sum of individual releases.
\end{itemize}

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Overlap--capacity duality on the battery lattice}
\begin{conjecture}
For a fixed population and label, $S\mapsto I(L;J_S)$ is a submodular function of the dial set, and the failure of modularity at a pair $\{i,j\}$ is exactly the pair co-information of that pair conditioned on the rest of the battery.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the width-$k$ identity is the telescoping of pairwise co-informations along any ordering of the battery, so modularity defects must localise at pairs conditioned on their predecessors. \textbf{Caution:} a three-dial parity battery already refutes naive submodularity --- two of its pairs carry zero capacity while the triple carries one bit. The sharp form to test is submodularity of $S\mapsto H(J_S)$ together with a supermodularity regime for capacity on batteries with pairwise independent readouts.

\subsection{Synergy ceiling in terms of unused code capacity}
\begin{conjecture}
At width $k$,
\[
\Delta(S)\;\le\;\sum_{i\in S}\big(H(f_i)-I(L;f_i)\big)\;-\;(k-1)\big(H(L)-I(L;J_S)\big),
\]
i.e.\ synergy is funded by unused per-dial code capacity and further taxed by residual label uncertainty.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that $\Delta\le\TC_L$ is proved, and $\TC_L$ is itself bounded by the conditional entropies of the readouts, which are the unused code capacities; the remaining half is the tax term.

\subsection{Further directions}
Predicting $\Dep_L$ from the Galois-theoretic type of the dials would upgrade the law from descriptive to predictive; bias-corrected estimators for $\Delta$ with valid confidence intervals are needed for the small-sample regimes in which batteries are measured; the identities should survive verbatim for differential entropies under mild regularity; and, given a catalogue and a budget $k$, characterising the optimal battery is a concrete optimisation target bracketed by Theorems~\ref{thm:kovl} and the width-$k$ ceiling.

\section{Conclusion}

Additivity of measurement capacity is not a law but a coincidence, and its exact condition is now known: two instruments read against a common label are additive precisely when their label-conditional dependence equals their unconditional dependence. The defect equals the difference of these two nonnegative quantities, hence is unsigned; it is bounded below by the shared structure of the readouts and above by their coding capacity; it equals a full marginal in the redundant extreme and the full label entropy in the maximally synergistic extreme; and every statement survives to arbitrary battery width with total correlation replacing pairwise dependence, sharp at every width. Two instruments pointed at the same world are never two independent instruments; the exact amount by which they fail to be, split into the part that duplicates and the part that combines, is the content of this theory.

